    "reason": "The English lemma 'senior' (noun) typically refers to a person (e.g., an older person, a person of higher rank/experience, a final-year student). The Japanese expression '高級' (こうきゅう) primarily means 'high-class' or 'high-grade' (referring to quality or level of things/services). While JMdict lists 'high-ranking' and 'high-level' for '高級', it does not naturally stand alone as a noun to mean 'a senior person'. Using '高級' for 'senior' (noun) would be semantically incorrect and unnatural for a learner, as it would imply 'a high-class item/person' rather than 'a person of higher rank/experience/age'.",
